var e={code:{title:`Florida DBPR: Building Code and official code access`,url:`https://www2.myfloridalicense.com/building-codes-and-standards/building-code/`},hillsborough:{title:`Hillsborough County: residential alterations submission checklist`,url:`https://hcfl.gov/businesses/hillsgovhub/residential-and-mobile-home-checklists/residential-alterations-renovations`},pinellas:{title:`Pinellas County: permitting guide`,url:`https://pinellas.gov/permitting-guide/`},flood:{title:`Pinellas County: flood information and substantial improvement rules`,url:`https://pinellas.gov/your-guide-to-flood-information-in-pinellas-county`},coastal:{title:`Florida DEP: Coastal Construction Control Line permitting`,url:`https://floridadep.gov/rcp/coastal-construction-control-line/content/apply-coastal-construction-control-line-cccl`},condo:{title:`Florida Statutes 718.113: condominium maintenance and common-element alterations`,url:`https://www.leg.state.fl.us/statutes/index.cfm?App_mode=Display_Statute&URL=0700-0799/0718/Sections/0718.113.html`},hoa:{title:`Florida Statutes 720.3035: architectural control and parcel improvements`,url:`https://wwwo.leg.state.fl.us/Statutes/index.cfm?App_mode=Display_Statute&URL=0700-0799/0720/Sections/0720.3035.html`},inspections:{title:`Florida DBPR: milestone inspections and structural integrity reserve studies`,url:`https://condos.myfloridalicense.com/inspections/`},historic:{title:`City of Tampa: architectural review and historic preservation`,url:`https://www.tampa.gov/historic-preservation`}},t=[{slug:`florida-building-permit-process-homeowners`,category:`Permits & codes`,service:`projectmanagement`,city:`riverview-fl`,image:`materials`,title:`Florida Building Permits: A Homeowner Process Guide`,description:`Follow the Florida building permit process from jurisdiction checks and plan review to inspections, revisions and project closeout.`,answer:`Begin with the property address and a written scope. Confirm the permitting authority, identify the required plans and trade permits, submit a coordinated application, resolve review comments, and complete the required inspections and closeout.`,sections:[{heading:`Confirm who reviews your property`,paragraphs:[`A postal city does not reliably identify the building department. A home with a Tampa mailing address may be outside city limits. Ask which agency has jurisdiction over the parcel before creating an application account or paying for drawings.`,`Prepare a one-page description of the project: rooms affected, walls changing, new openings, utility moves and any exterior work. Use that same description when speaking with the designer, contractor and permit office. Conflicting scopes often lead to conflicting instructions.`]},{heading:`Submit one coordinated package`,paragraphs:[`Ask the project team to create a document register with a responsible person for each drawing and supporting form. The Hillsborough residential alterations checklist is one local example; another municipality may request different documentation.`,`Keep reviewer comments alongside the response and revised drawing number. When a design changes during review, check whether related electrical, plumbing or mechanical information must also change. Submission is a review step; it does not authorize construction.`]},{heading:`Build inspection stops into the schedule`,paragraphs:[`After permit issuance, review the approved scope, conditions and inspection sequence with the contractor. Plan work so that items requiring inspection remain accessible. Do not base cabinet installation or wall closure on an assumed inspection date.`,`At closeout, ask for the permit status and applicable final approvals. Keep the approved plans, revisions and inspection records with the home records. If work remains incomplete, assign someone to resolve it before treating the project as finished.`]}],checklist:[`Confirm the parcel jurisdiction.`,`Create a scope and document register.`,`Track review responses and revisions.`,`Collect final inspection and permit records.`],faqs:[{question:`Does submitting a permit application allow construction to start?`,answer:`No. Confirm issuance, conditions and any other required approvals with the authority before starting the permitted work.`},{question:`How long will a Florida permit take?`,answer:`Timing depends on the authority, scope, application completeness and review responses. Build a project-specific schedule rather than relying on a statewide estimate.`}],sources:[e.hillsborough,e.pinellas]},{slug:`florida-building-code-remodeling-existing-homes`,category:`Permits & codes`,service:`remodeling`,city:`tampa-fl`,image:`interior`,title:`Florida Building Code: Planning an Existing-Home Remodel`,description:`Understand how Florida building codes affect remodeling plans, existing conditions, trade coordination and inspections before selecting finishes.`,answer:`A remodel needs a scope-specific code review. Ask the design and construction team to confirm the applicable code edition and requirements for the existing building, proposed alterations and affected systems with the local building department.`,sections:[{heading:`Describe the work before asking what code applies`,paragraphs:[`Replacing finishes, changing a floor plan and adding living space create different design questions. Explain the physical work instead of relying on labels such as cosmetic renovation. Include changes that may be hidden, such as a relocated drain, exhaust duct or electrical circuit.`,`Use the official DBPR code access page to locate Florida code resources. Have the responsible professional identify the provisions used for the permit package. An old drawing or a product brochure does not establish which requirements govern a new application.`]},{heading:`Resolve existing conditions before final selections`,paragraphs:[`Ask which measurements and observations can be completed before demolition. A proposed open kitchen may need a structural review; a new shower layout may need a drainage review. Do not assume a wall is nonbearing or a floor can accept a new opening from a photograph.`,`Create a decision log for conditions that cannot be verified until work is opened. Record the discovery, proposed response, cost implication and required approval. This makes changes easier to evaluate before they affect several trades.`]},{heading:`Coordinate details across trades`,paragraphs:[`For example, a kitchen hood affects cabinet dimensions, a wall or roof opening, electrical supply and the exhaust route. Ask one person to check that the drawings and installation instructions describe the same solution.`,`Keep approved substitutions in the project file. If an appliance or window changes after review, ask whether the revised selection changes the plans or permit requirements. Resolve that question before installation rather than at the final inspection.`]}],checklist:[`Confirm the applicable code edition for the application.`,`List structural and trade changes.`,`Record unresolved existing conditions.`,`Review substitutions before installation.`],faqs:[{question:`Does remodeling mean the entire house must be rebuilt to current code?`,answer:`That is not a conclusion to draw from the word remodel. Applicable requirements depend on the work, existing conditions and the code review for that project.`},{question:`Can a contractor promise code approval before reviewing the site?`,answer:`A proposal can describe an intended approach, but approval depends on the actual documents, conditions and authority review.`}],sources:[e.code]},{slug:`pinellas-flood-zone-remodeling-substantial-improvement`,category:`Coastal & local planning`,service:`remodeling`,city:`st-pete-beach-fl`,image:`exterior`,title:`Pinellas Flood-Zone Remodels and the 50% Rule`,description:`Plan a Pinellas flood-zone remodel with early checks for substantial improvement, building valuation, prior work and floodplain documentation.`,answer:`Before pricing a flood-zone renovation, ask the local floodplain administrator how substantial improvement and substantial damage rules apply to the property. The calculation concerns the structure and eligible project costs, with local requirements that must be confirmed.`,sections:[{heading:`Ask for a determination before committing to the scope`,paragraphs:[`Pinellas County explains that substantial improvement rules can affect work on floodplain buildings. The familiar 50% description is a starting point for questions, not an automatic allowance for a particular house. Ask the municipality or county serving the parcel which calculation and documentation it uses.`,`Request the accepted building valuation method and the treatment of previous work. Do not calculate a renovation allowance from the property purchase price, which includes more than the structure. Obtain the authority response before using a number in a construction budget.`]},{heading:`Make the cost package transparent`,paragraphs:[`Create a room-by-room work inventory with the contractor. Separate retained assemblies, proposed replacements and optional changes so the reviewer can understand the whole scope. Ask the authority which labor and material costs must be represented, including owner-supplied items.`,`If storm repairs and discretionary upgrades are being considered together, present both clearly. Dividing invoices or applications should never be treated as a way to bypass the applicable review. Ask how the jurisdiction evaluates related work and revisions.`]},{heading:`Compare realistic paths forward`,paragraphs:[`If floodplain compliance changes the project feasibility, ask the design team to compare alternatives before ordering finishes. An adjusted scope, a different building approach or additional evaluation may be necessary; the authority must determine what is acceptable.`,`Keep the valuation, determination, approved scope and subsequent changes together. A late expansion can change the review assumptions. Make floodplain review a decision point in the schedule rather than a document added immediately before construction.`]}],checklist:[`Identify the floodplain administrator.`,`Request the accepted structure valuation method.`,`Disclose the complete scope and relevant prior work.`,`Obtain the determination before major commitments.`],faqs:[{question:`Is the 50% rule based on the value of my land and house together?`,answer:`Do not use the combined property price. Ask the authority for the accepted structure valuation and project cost calculation.`},{question:`Does every Pinellas municipality use identical procedures?`,answer:`Confirm the rules and forms for the agency serving your parcel; county guidance does not replace a municipal determination.`}],sources:[e.flood,e.pinellas]},{slug:`clearwater-beach-coastal-construction-planning`,category:`Coastal & local planning`,service:`new-construction`,city:`clearwater-beach-fl`,image:`exterior`,title:`Building Near Clearwater Beach: Coastal Site Planning`,description:`Review coastal construction questions near Clearwater Beach, including CCCL jurisdiction, flood elevations, access and material exposure.`,answer:`Coastal construction starts with the parcel, survey and proposed work. Check local zoning and floodplain requirements, and determine whether Florida DEP Coastal Construction Control Line review applies before finalizing a beachfront design.`,sections:[{heading:`Separate coastal jurisdiction from local building review`,paragraphs:[`DEP explains that construction and excavation seaward of the Coastal Construction Control Line generally require its permit unless exempt. The line identifies a regulated coastal area; it is not simply a universal building setback. Ask DEP about the actual parcel and activity.`,`For a Clearwater Beach property, have the team map the site constraints before choosing an addition footprint or exterior improvement. A local building permit does not by itself settle a separate state coastal permitting question. Record the approvals needed and who will obtain them.`]},{heading:`Plan around exposure and elevation`,paragraphs:[`Ask the designer to evaluate the property-specific flood and wind conditions and their effect on the structure, openings and equipment locations. Nearby homes are useful context but do not establish the design requirements for your lot.`,`Discuss salt exposure and maintenance when choosing exterior hardware, coatings and equipment. Request manufacturer information for the intended environment. A finish that looks suitable indoors may require a different maintenance commitment near the water.`]},{heading:`Make construction access part of design`,paragraphs:[`Walk the delivery route before ordering large assemblies. Discuss available staging, protection of adjacent property, waste removal and how workers will enter the site. A tight lot can change the practical sequence even when the floor plan is straightforward.`,`Ask how temporary protection will be maintained between work stages and during unsuitable weather. Keep the owner, design team and contractor aligned on the approval path before making nonrefundable product commitments. Resolve coastal questions early enough to influence the plan.`]}],checklist:[`Review a current survey and parcel constraints.`,`Confirm whether DEP coastal review applies.`,`Coordinate flood and wind design questions.`,`Plan delivery, staging and weather protection.`],faqs:[{question:`Does every Clearwater Beach remodel need a CCCL permit?`,answer:`No blanket answer applies. DEP jurisdiction, the activity and any exemption must be checked for the particular property.`},{question:`Can I use a neighboring home as my permitted design?`,answer:`A nearby home does not establish the approvals or engineering requirements for your lot and proposed work.`}],sources:[e.coastal,e.flood]},{slug:`tampa-historic-home-additions-renovation-approvals`,category:`Coastal & local planning`,service:`additions`,city:`south-tampa-fl`,image:`exterior`,title:`Tampa Historic Homes: Renovation and Addition Approvals`,description:`Plan a Tampa historic-home renovation by checking designation, exterior design review, permit coordination and documentation before ordering.`,answer:`Confirm whether a Tampa property is a designated landmark or within a local historic district before planning exterior alterations. Ask the preservation office whether a Certificate of Appropriateness is needed and how its review coordinates with the building permit.`,sections:[{heading:`Verify designation instead of guessing from age`,paragraphs:[`Tampa manages architectural review for local historic districts and landmarks. A home being old does not by itself tell you its review status. Start with the exact address and ask which designation and design guidelines apply.`,`Describe the proposed exterior work with photos: windows, doors, porch details, roofing or an addition. Tampa identifies a Certificate of Appropriateness process for applicable projects. Ask which review path fits the work before treating a contractor quote as the final design.`]},{heading:`Present the existing home and proposal together`,paragraphs:[`Create an organized photo set of the street elevation and the areas that will change. Label retained details and proposed replacements. For an addition, show how the new volume relates to the existing building rather than presenting an isolated room layout.`,`Ask the design professional to coordinate dimensions, product profiles and visible materials. If a preferred window or cladding system is unavailable, review the alternative before buying it. A substitution can affect both appearance review and the construction documents.`]},{heading:`Keep design and construction approvals aligned`,paragraphs:[`Put the preservation review and permit steps on the same schedule. Ask who responds to each office and which drawings represent the currently approved design. An informal conversation is useful preparation, but keep the actual decisions and conditions in writing.`,`Before demolition, walk the site with the team and identify details to protect, salvage or document. Use photographs and labels so protection expectations are clear to every trade. If an unexpected condition changes the exterior proposal, pause that decision for the appropriate review.`]}],checklist:[`Verify the exact designation and review office.`,`Gather labeled exterior photos.`,`Coordinate visible product selections.`,`Keep preservation and permit approvals together.`],faqs:[{question:`Is every older house in South Tampa historically designated?`,answer:`No. Verify the designation and review requirements for the address instead of relying on age or neighborhood name.`},{question:`Does historic design approval replace the building permit?`,answer:`Treat them as distinct approvals and ask the city how to coordinate both for the proposed work.`}],sources:[e.historic]},{slug:`florida-condo-renovation-association-approval-process`,category:`Condominiums & HOAs`,service:`condo-renovations`,city:`clearwater-fl`,image:`interior`,title:`Florida Condo Renovations: The Association Approval Process`,description:`Prepare a Florida condo renovation application with scope drawings, association requirements, contractor documents and a clear approval sequence.`,answer:`Request the condominium alteration rules before finalizing a remodel. Submit the scope, plans and contractor documents required by your association, resolve questions about shared property, and obtain the relevant written approvals and permits before work starts.`,sections:[{heading:`Start with the current alteration package`,paragraphs:[`Ask the manager for the declaration, applicable rules and current renovation application. Create a list of required submissions, decision makers and conditions for starting. Do not assume that a former owner approval or a neighboring unit project applies to yours.`,`A useful submission describes demolition, floor finishes, utility changes and any proposed openings. Mark the unit number and drawing revision on each document. Ask the manager to confirm receipt and identify missing items so the review does not depend on an incomplete email chain.`]},{heading:`Identify work that touches shared property`,paragraphs:[`Florida Statutes 718.113 addresses common-element maintenance and alterations. Before changing a slab, riser, exterior opening or balcony, ask the association to confirm ownership and approval responsibilities from the governing documents.`,`Use a responsibility table: owner work, association work, design review and access coordination. A kitchen drain relocation is not only a cabinet decision if the route crosses shared construction. Have the appropriate professional review the actual assembly before accepting a layout.`]},{heading:`Turn approval into a workable start plan`,paragraphs:[`Separate association approval from government permit issuance. Track both in writing and confirm any remaining conditions. Do not schedule demolition solely because one part of the process is complete.`,`Before the crew arrives, confirm access, permitted work hours, delivery reservations and required protection. These are building-specific arrangements; request the actual rules instead of relying on generic condo renovation advice. Share the approved work package with the site supervisor and keep revisions controlled.`]}],checklist:[`Obtain the current alteration rules.`,`Submit a labeled scope and drawings.`,`Resolve common-element responsibilities.`,`Confirm approvals, permits and access conditions.`],faqs:[{question:`Does a building permit replace condo board approval?`,answer:`No. Government permitting and association authorization address different responsibilities. Confirm both for your project.`},{question:`Can a condo owner move any interior wall?`,answer:`Do not assume that interior location makes a wall owner-controlled or nonstructural. Review the documents and construction before deciding.`}],sources:[e.condo,e.pinellas]},{slug:`condo-flooring-bathroom-shared-systems-florida`,category:`Condominiums & HOAs`,service:`condo-renovations`,city:`st-petersburg-fl`,image:`bathroom`,title:`Condo Floors and Bathrooms: Shared-System Planning`,description:`Plan condo flooring and bathroom work around acoustic assemblies, waterproofing, shared plumbing, slab restrictions and association review.`,answer:`A condo floor or bathroom remodel should be planned around the complete assembly and the building rules. Confirm acoustic documentation, waterproofing details, shared plumbing coordination and restrictions on slab work before purchasing materials.`,sections:[{heading:`Get the floor assembly accepted before buying tile`,paragraphs:[`Ask the association what acoustic information it requires for the proposed flooring assembly. A rating printed on an underlayment package may not describe performance in the actual building. Submit the finish, underlayment and installation information together.`,`Confirm substrate preparation, transitions and finished heights with the contractor. Replacing carpet with hard flooring can affect neighboring units as well as door clearances. Obtain the association response to the proposed assembly before placing an order that cannot be returned.`]},{heading:`Coordinate bathrooms beyond the unit boundary`,paragraphs:[`Locate the existing drain and supply arrangement before moving fixtures. Ask who may authorize shutdowns and who notifies affected residents. A shared riser should not be treated as an ordinary in-unit plumbing change.`,`Have the team document the shower waterproofing, drain and floor transitions as one system. Agree how concealed work will be recorded and inspected. Protect adjacent spaces and identify an emergency contact for an unexpected leak during construction.`]},{heading:`Do not drill or cut an unknown slab`,paragraphs:[`Before proposed coring, anchoring or drain relocation, obtain building information and a qualified evaluation of the affected structure. A post-tensioned slab or other structural system needs project-specific direction. Stop an unverified penetration rather than treating it as routine demolition.`,`Florida condominium law and the declaration help establish common-element responsibilities. Ask the manager and design professional which permissions and details apply to the slab and shared systems. Keep the approved instructions available to every trade working in that area.`]}],checklist:[`Submit the full flooring assembly.`,`Confirm shared water shutdown procedures.`,`Resolve waterproofing and drain details.`,`Obtain direction before slab penetrations.`],faqs:[{question:`Is there one sound rating accepted by every Florida condominium?`,answer:`Do not assume a universal association threshold. Check the building requirements and the applicable code with the project team.`},{question:`Can a shower drain always be moved in a condo?`,answer:`Feasibility depends on the structure, plumbing route, ownership boundaries and approvals. Review those conditions before selecting the layout.`}],sources:[e.code,e.condo]},{slug:`florida-hoa-architectural-review-home-improvements`,category:`Condominiums & HOAs`,service:`additions`,city:`wesley-chapel-fl`,image:`exterior`,title:`Florida HOA Architectural Review: From Plans to Approval`,description:`Prepare an HOA architectural review application for a Florida addition or exterior improvement, with drawings, selections and written decisions.`,answer:`Obtain the HOA declaration and authorized architectural standards, identify the application requirements, and submit a complete improvement package. Track the written decision and conditions separately from building permits and zoning review.`,sections:[{heading:`Find the standards that apply to the change`,paragraphs:[`Florida Statutes 720.3035 ties HOA architectural control to the authority in the declaration and authorized standards. Request those documents and the current application instead of relying on a verbal summary from a neighbor.`,`For a Wesley Chapel addition, create a question list covering the proposed footprint, exterior materials, colors and roof relationship. Ask the reviewer which items require drawings or samples. If requirements appear inconsistent, request clarification of the applicable provision before redesigning.`]},{heading:`Make the submission easy to understand`,paragraphs:[`Provide an existing-condition photo set, a survey or site drawing where requested, and elevations showing the exterior change. Label dimensions and selections consistently. A clear package helps reviewers understand exactly what they are being asked to approve.`,`Separate confirmed selections from alternatives. If a roof material or paint color is still undecided, say so and ask how final selection approval works. Keep one dated package so a later contractor substitution does not silently change the approved project.`]},{heading:`Track conditions and changes in writing`,paragraphs:[`Ask how receipt, completeness, review and the final decision are documented under the community process. Do not assume every association has the same deadline or that silence means approval. Keep the written response and any conditions with the contract documents.`,`Government permits remain a separate part of the project. Before work starts, compare the HOA-approved exterior with the permitted design and resolve differences. If a change becomes necessary during construction, ask whether resubmission is required before installing it.`]}],checklist:[`Request the declaration and architectural standards.`,`Submit a consistent drawing and selection package.`,`Keep written decisions and conditions.`,`Review changes before installation.`],faqs:[{question:`Is an HOA approval the same as a permit?`,answer:`No. HOA authorization does not establish compliance with government permitting or zoning requirements.`},{question:`Can I use the approval issued to my neighbor?`,answer:`Use it as context only. Your property, documents and proposed improvement still need the applicable review.`}],sources:[e.hoa]},{slug:`condo-renovation-logistics-elevators-neighbors-florida`,category:`Condominiums & HOAs`,service:`condo-renovations`,city:`clearwater-beach-fl`,image:`materials`,title:`Condo Renovation Logistics: Elevators, Access and Neighbors`,description:`Organize a Florida condo renovation with a building-specific plan for elevators, deliveries, work hours, water shutdowns and common-area protection.`,answer:`Build the condo construction schedule around the actual building rules. Confirm elevator reservations, delivery dimensions, work hours, common-area protection, debris routes and shared-service shutdowns before setting the start date.`,sections:[{heading:`Measure the route from truck to unit`,paragraphs:[`Walk the entire route with the site supervisor: loading area, doors, elevator, corridors and unit entry. Check the size and handling needs of countertops, cabinets and shower glass before fabrication. A product fitting the room does not mean it can reach the room.`,`Ask management which routes are available and what reservations or supervision are required. Record who books the elevator and who receives deliveries. Do not treat a reservation request as a confirmed delivery slot.`]},{heading:`Agree on protection and daily operating rules`,paragraphs:[`Request the building-specific requirements for floor protection, elevator padding, dust control and debris removal. Ask whether insurance certificates, contractor registration, deposits or other paperwork are required; these are questions to verify, not universal fees or rules.`,`Photograph existing common-area conditions with management where appropriate. Give the crew a short site sheet with the approved hours, access procedure and contact names. Include a daily cleanup routine so materials and waste do not obstruct shared circulation.`]},{heading:`Coordinate disruptions before promising completion`,paragraphs:[`Schedule noisy work, inspections and any shared water shutdown through the proper contacts. Ask management how resident notices are issued and what information must be supplied. Avoid announcing a shutdown until authorization and trade attendance are confirmed.`,`Track logistics alongside construction dependencies. A missed elevator slot can affect material arrival and the next trade even when installation work is ready. Include a plan for delayed deliveries and keep one contact responsible for communicating schedule changes to the owner and manager.`]}],checklist:[`Measure and approve the delivery route.`,`Confirm reservations and building paperwork.`,`Document protection and cleanup responsibilities.`,`Coordinate shutdowns and resident notices.`],faqs:[{question:`Do all Florida condos require a renovation deposit?`,answer:`Requirements vary. Ask for the current building rules and written terms for any requested deposit.`},{question:`Can the crew work whenever the city allows construction noise?`,answer:`Check both local requirements and the association work rules. The permitted project schedule must account for the applicable restrictions.`}],sources:[e.condo]},{slug:`condo-milestone-inspections-sirs-remodel-planning`,category:`Condominiums & HOAs`,service:`condo-renovations`,city:`st-pete-beach-fl`,image:`exterior`,title:`Condo Milestone Inspections, SIRS and Your Remodel`,description:`Understand how Florida condo milestone inspections, reserve studies and association repairs can influence the timing and scope of a unit remodel.`,answer:`Before a major unit renovation, ask management about building inspections, planned repairs and access needs. A milestone inspection evaluates structural condition; a structural integrity reserve study supports repair and replacement funding. Neither replaces your renovation approvals.`,sections:[{heading:`Understand the two different building records`,paragraphs:[`DBPR describes milestone inspections as structural evaluations and SIRS as reserve planning for specified building components. Ask management for the relevant records and the status of any resulting repair program rather than assuming the two documents are interchangeable.`,`Applicability and deadlines depend on the building and current law. Confirm those questions with management and the responsible professionals. A unit remodeling proposal should not be presented as proof that the association has met its inspection or reserve obligations.`]},{heading:`Ask what the association will repair next`,paragraphs:[`Before replacing finishes, ask about planned work on windows, waterproofing, plumbing or exterior assemblies that could require unit access. The practical question is whether your new work may need to be opened or protected during a building project.`,`Create a coordination list for overlapping areas. For example, a new bathroom finish may need a different sequence if the association plans nearby plumbing repairs. Ask the project teams to confirm responsibilities, access and restoration expectations before finalizing the unit scope.`]},{heading:`Separate project decisions and budgets`,paragraphs:[`Keep the unit renovation proposal separate from association assessments and building repair contracts. Ask which costs are owner scope and which decisions belong to the association. Do not assume a private remodel can resolve an identified common-element condition.`,`If a report raises a condition relevant to the proposed work, bring it to the appropriate licensed professional before proceeding. Use the resulting direction to adjust the schedule or details. Record what must happen first instead of working around an unresolved building issue.`]}],checklist:[`Request relevant inspection and reserve records.`,`Ask about planned repairs and unit access.`,`Coordinate overlapping work before new finishes.`,`Separate unit scope from association obligations.`],faqs:[{question:`Does every condo kitchen remodel trigger a milestone inspection?`,answer:`Milestone obligations concern qualifying buildings and statutory criteria. A kitchen remodel does not by itself establish that obligation; confirm the building status with management.`},{question:`Does a SIRS approve the safety of my renovation design?`,answer:`No. A reserve study serves a different purpose from the design review, association approval and permits for the proposed alteration.`}],sources:[e.inspections]}].map(e=>({...e,date:`2026-10-23`})),n={title:`City of Tampa: residential new construction`,url:`https://www.tampa.gov/construction-services/residential-permits/new-construction`},r={title:`Hillsborough County: residential and mobile home checklists`,url:`https://hcfl.gov/businesses/hillsgovhub/residential-and-mobile-home-checklists`},i={title:`Hillsborough County: single-family addition intake requirements`,url:`https://hcfl.gov/businesses/hillsgovhub/residential-and-mobile-home-checklists/residential-single-family-addition-requirements/single-family-addition-general-intake-requirements`},a=[{slug:`new-home-construction-tampa-planning`,category:`New construction`,service:`new-construction`,city:`tampa-fl`,image:`exterior`,title:`New Home Construction in Tampa: Where to Start`,description:`Plan new home construction in Tampa with a practical guide to lot feasibility, design coordination, site costs, inspections and builder proposals.`,answer:`Start new home construction in Tampa by confirming the permitting jurisdiction and investigating the lot before finalizing the floor plan. Coordinate the survey, utility information, site constraints, design documents and construction budget so the proposed home fits both the property and your priorities.`,sections:[{heading:`Check the Tampa lot before choosing the floor plan`,paragraphs:[`A listing address and a conceptual rendering do not establish what can be built. Ask the design team to review a current survey, recorded easements, access, zoning and the existing site conditions. Confirm the authority responsible for the specific parcel; a Tampa mailing address alone does not settle jurisdiction.`,`For an infill concept in South Tampa, Seminole Heights or West Tampa, discuss how deliveries, material storage and equipment will reach the building area. These are planning examples, not findings about every property in those neighborhoods. A workable footprint must leave room for construction activity as well as the completed home.`]},{heading:`Separate the house budget from site preparation`,paragraphs:[`Ask for a budget that distinguishes the building from demolition, clearing, earthwork, utility connections and exterior improvements. Identify which items are based on actual documents and which remain allowances. A house price that excludes site preparation cannot be compared directly with a proposal that includes it.`,`Create a decision log with the assumption, person responsible and date for resolving it. For example, an unknown utility connection should become an assigned investigation rather than an unspoken expectation. Review drainage and foundation questions with the appropriate professionals before treating a preliminary estimate as a construction commitment.`]},{heading:`Coordinate structure, layout and systems together`,paragraphs:[`Agree on room sizes, furniture layouts and daily circulation before refining finishes. A kitchen island affects walkways and electrical coordination; a large opening affects structural design; mechanical equipment needs space and service access. Resolving these relationships on drawings is easier than changing completed work.`,`Ask how the team will coordinate architectural, structural and building-system information. Keep a dated selection schedule for windows, doors, cabinetry and equipment. Confirm product dimensions and procurement assumptions before releasing dependent work, and record the effect of any later substitution.`]},{heading:`Build a schedule around approvals and verifiable milestones`,paragraphs:[`The City of Tampa publishes a residential new-construction permitting entry point. Use the current authority checklist for the actual project rather than assuming another home used the same submission requirements. Ask who submits documents, answers review comments and tracks revisions.`,`Tie the construction schedule to measurable milestones: site readiness, foundation work, enclosure, rough systems, finishes and closeout. Discuss inspections before work is concealed. Request final approval records and operating information at handover; a finished appearance alone does not establish that permit closeout is complete.`]}],checklist:[`Confirm jurisdiction and obtain a current survey.`,`Review access, utilities, drainage and design constraints.`,`Separate site costs, allowances and building scope.`,`Assign design decisions and approval responsibilities.`,`Include inspections and closeout in the schedule.`],faqs:[{question:`Can I price a new Tampa home from a floor plan alone?`,answer:`A floor plan can support an early discussion, but a useful construction proposal also needs site information, specifications, engineering scope and clear exclusions. Ask which missing items could change the price.`},{question:`Should I choose a builder before buying a Tampa lot?`,answer:`Involving a builder and design professionals during due diligence can help identify access, site-work and coordination questions before purchase. Confirm any purchase deadlines with your transaction advisers.`}],sources:[n,{title:`City of Tampa: construction services questions and inspections`,url:`https://www.tampa.gov/construction-services/how-do-i`}]},{slug:`south-tampa-home-addition-design-checklist`,category:`Additions`,service:`additions`,city:`south-tampa-fl`,image:`exterior`,title:`South Tampa Home Additions: A Design Checklist`,description:`Plan a South Tampa home addition around existing structure, room connections, roof design, site access and a clearly defined construction scope.`,answer:`A successful South Tampa home addition starts with two checks: whether the site can accommodate the proposed work and how the new rooms will connect to the existing house. Evaluate structure, roof transitions, drainage, utilities and daily circulation before choosing finishes or committing to a footprint.`,sections:[{heading:`Define the problem the addition must solve`,paragraphs:[`Write a short brief describing who will use the new space and how it should work. A primary suite, family room and multigenerational living area create different needs for privacy, storage and connections to the rest of the house. Measure furniture and map movement through adjacent rooms.`,`For a home in Palma Ceia, Ballast Point or the Davis Islands area, use the actual parcel and existing house as the starting point. Neighborhood names do not establish setbacks, flood conditions or approval requirements. Ask the design team to investigate the address before relying on a sketch.`]},{heading:`Investigate the connection to the existing house`,paragraphs:[`An addition changes more than the room at its edge. Ask how the team will evaluate existing walls, foundations, roof framing and building systems where new work connects. Retain space in the budget and schedule to investigate concealed conditions rather than assuming existing construction matches old drawings.`,`Request drawings that explain the roof connection and how water will be managed at transitions. Discuss ceiling heights, floor elevations, exterior openings and finish transitions together. A small mismatch can affect several trades, so identify the responsible designer and the sequence for resolving details.`]},{heading:`Compare a rear addition with an interior reconfiguration`,paragraphs:[`A rear addition may provide the desired room while leaving the main living area intact, but the route to that room still matters. Compare it with an alternative that reconfigures some existing space. Evaluate usable room area, privacy and furniture placement rather than added square footage alone.`,`Treat an upstairs option as a separate feasibility exercise. Existing structural capacity, stairs and system changes need professional evaluation. Do not assume building upward avoids site or approval questions, or that it will be cheaper than extending the footprint. Ask for comparable assumptions for each option.`]},{heading:`Plan site access and life during construction`,paragraphs:[`Identify where crews will enter, where deliveries can unload and how occupied rooms will be protected. Confirm temporary separation, access to bathrooms and any planned utility interruptions. If the home stays occupied, agree on which activities require residents to leave the work area.`,`Use the relevant permitting checklist for the property and discuss any additional reviews identified by the project team. Before starting, define restoration of disturbed landscaping, fencing and access routes. Include the work needed to make existing rooms complete where the addition connects, not just the new room finishes.`]}],checklist:[`Write a room-use brief with furniture dimensions.`,`Verify parcel constraints before approving a footprint.`,`Investigate structural and roof connections.`,`Compare alternatives using the same scope assumptions.`,`Define occupied-home logistics and restoration work.`],faqs:[{question:`Is a second-story addition easier than a rear addition in South Tampa?`,answer:`Neither option is automatically easier. A second story requires evaluation of the existing structure and stair layout; a rear addition needs review of the available site and its connection to the home.`},{question:`Should an addition match the existing roof?`,answer:`Appearance matters, but the design must also resolve structure, drainage and weather protection. Review the transition details with the design team before selecting the final exterior treatment.`}],sources:[n,i]},{slug:`carrollwood-lutz-whole-home-renovation-planning`,category:`Remodeling`,service:`remodeling`,city:`carrollwood-fl`,image:`interior`,title:`Carrollwood and Lutz Home Renovation Planning`,description:`Plan a home renovation in Carrollwood or Lutz with clear priorities for existing conditions, layout changes, systems, selections and occupied-home logistics.`,answer:`Plan a Carrollwood or Lutz home renovation by documenting existing conditions and separating layout needs from finish upgrades. Coordinate structural questions, electrical and plumbing work, heating and cooling, and material selections before setting a demolition date. Verify the permitting authority for the exact address.`,sections:[{heading:`Document how the home works today`,paragraphs:[`Walk through the house and record specific problems: a narrow kitchen walkway, a bathroom without usable storage, a dark hallway or a room that cannot accommodate its intended furniture. Add photographs, approximate dimensions and a note about when each issue affects daily life.`,`In Carrollwood and Lutz, avoid making assumptions about a house from the community name or apparent age. Gather any available plans, permit history and records of previous improvements. Ask the project team which existing conditions need investigation before a proposed layout can be priced responsibly.`]},{heading:`Resolve wall changes before cabinetry and finishes`,paragraphs:[`An open kitchen concept should begin with a review of what the walls contain and support. A wall may carry structural loads or contain plumbing, wiring or mechanical components. Have the appropriate professionals evaluate changes before treating removal as a cosmetic task.`,`Compare a full wall removal with a smaller opening or a revised cabinet layout. Place furniture and appliances on each option to check clearances and storage. Document why the chosen option performs better so that a later finish decision does not quietly undo the layout goal.`]},{heading:`Coordinate systems with the scope of renovation`,paragraphs:[`Ask the project team how planned rooms affect lighting, outlets, plumbing locations and heating or cooling distribution. Record what is included in the proposal and what is subject to further investigation. A new fixture does not by itself resolve a system problem elsewhere in the house.`,`Check manufacturer installation information when selecting products. Flooring transitions, appliance clearances, bathroom assemblies and cabinet dimensions must work together. Keep one approved selection record with product identifiers and required dates, then review substitutions before ordering or installation.`]},{heading:`Choose phases based on daily use and dependencies`,paragraphs:[`If you intend to remain at home, identify a working bathroom, food preparation arrangements and a route that avoids construction activity. Review planned shutdowns and the conditions for access to each work zone. Ask how dust separation and end-of-day cleanup will be handled.`,`Phasing can preserve some access, but repeating protection, mobilization or finish transitions may add work. Compare a phased approach with a continuous work period using the same scope. Obtain the applicable renovation requirements from the permitting authority and allow for inspections before closing walls.`]}],checklist:[`Document practical problems and existing records.`,`Evaluate wall changes before finalizing cabinetry.`,`Coordinate systems and product installation needs.`,`Compare phased and continuous construction.`,`Confirm jurisdiction, inspections and daily access.`],faqs:[{question:`Can I live in my Carrollwood home during a renovation?`,answer:`That depends on the work zones, functioning facilities, utility interruptions and separation from construction. Agree on an occupancy plan with the contractor before scheduling demolition.`},{question:`Does a Lutz mailing address determine the permit office?`,answer:`Use the exact parcel to confirm jurisdiction. Mailing addresses and community names should not replace a check with the responsible local authority.`}],sources:[r,{title:`Hillsborough County: residential renovation submissions`,url:`https://hcfl.gov/businesses/hillsgovhub/residential-and-mobile-home-checklists/residential-alterations-renovations`}]},{slug:`brandon-riverview-building-lot-feasibility`,category:`New construction`,service:`new-construction`,city:`riverview-fl`,image:`materials`,title:`Building in Brandon or Riverview: Check the Lot First`,description:`Evaluate a building lot in Brandon or Riverview before design: review surveys, access, utilities, drainage, site-work allowances and construction logistics.`,answer:`Before building in Brandon or Riverview, investigate the parcel rather than relying on lot size or a listing description. Confirm legal and physical access, survey information, utilities, drainage questions and the permitting authority. Use the findings to define a buildable concept and a transparent site-work budget.`,sections:[{heading:`Turn the property information into a feasibility brief`,paragraphs:[`Collect the parcel identifier, survey, available title information and any existing improvement records. Ask the design team to identify information that is missing or needs updating. A boundary drawing, a listing sketch and a construction site plan serve different purposes; clarify which documents the next design decision requires.`,`In Brandon, Riverview and nearby Valrico, use local place names to describe location, not to infer a uniform set of site conditions. Two parcels nearby can differ in access, recorded restrictions and utility arrangements. Record findings for the specific address and keep unresolved assumptions visible.`]},{heading:`Confirm utilities and the route to the building area`,paragraphs:[`Ask how water, wastewater, electrical service and other required connections will reach the proposed building. Identify who verifies availability, capacity and connection scope. If a septic system is part of the proposal, discuss the required project-specific documentation before finalizing the layout.`,`Walk the intended delivery route with the project team. Consider equipment access, overhead obstructions, turning room and material storage. Define whether temporary access work or restoration is included. A lot can fit a house on paper while still needing a detailed plan for constructing it.`]},{heading:`Resolve drainage and foundation questions before pricing certainty`,paragraphs:[`Ask the relevant professionals what site, soil and elevation information they need. Discuss how the proposed home, driveway and outdoor surfaces interact with drainage. A dry visit to the site does not establish how it behaves during heavy rain, and nearby construction does not establish the correct foundation for your parcel.`,`Request a list of investigations and the design decisions each one supports. The goal is to replace assumptions with usable information before committing to dependent work. Do not treat a generic foundation or grading allowance as confirmation that the actual site design is complete.`]},{heading:`Compare proposals with a shared scope sheet`,paragraphs:[`Give each builder the same available documents and ask for a breakdown of building work, site preparation, utility connections, allowances and exclusions. Identify who carries the cost of additional investigation or a change in the site design. Keep contingency distinct from money already assigned to known work.`,`Hillsborough County provides residential checklists and addition submission guidance. Confirm which current checklist applies to the actual project and who will coordinate responses to review comments. Build a schedule that includes missing information, approvals, procurement and inspections before expecting a reliable start date.`]}],checklist:[`Gather parcel, survey and available property records.`,`Confirm jurisdiction and project submission requirements.`,`Investigate access and utility connections.`,`Assign drainage, soil and foundation questions.`,`Compare bids using one scope and allowance sheet.`],faqs:[{question:`Is the cheapest lot the cheapest place to build in Riverview?`,answer:`The purchase price is only one part of the project. Access, utilities, site preparation, design requirements and other parcel conditions can change the overall cost. Compare total project assumptions before choosing.`},{question:`What should I bring to a Brandon new-construction consultation?`,answer:`Bring the parcel address, available survey and records, a room-use brief, your target budget and any purchase or planning deadlines. List unknowns so the team can propose the next investigations.`}],sources:[r,i]}].map(e=>({...e,date:`2026-10-23`})),o={title:`Hillsborough County: residential alterations`,url:`https://hcfl.gov/businesses/hillsgovhub/residential-and-mobile-home-checklists/residential-alterations-renovations`},s={title:`City of Tampa: residential permits`,url:`https://www.tampa.gov/construction-services/residential-permits`},c={title:`Florida DBPR: how to verify a license`,url:`https://www2.myfloridalicense.com/how-to-verify-a-license/`},l={title:`ENERGY STAR: indoor air quality features`,url:`https://www.energystar.gov/newhomes/features-benefits/indoor-air-quality-features`},u={title:`Florida Division of Emergency Management: planning for your home`,url:`https://www.floridadisaster.org/planprepare/home`},d=[...a,...t,{slug:`kitchen-remodel-planning-tampa`,category:`Kitchens`,service:`kitchens-bathrooms`,city:`tampa-fl`,image:`interior`,title:`How to Plan a Kitchen Remodel in Tampa`,description:`Plan a Tampa kitchen remodel around layout, cabinetry, appliances and a clear scope. Learn which decisions to make before construction starts.`,answer:`Start a Tampa kitchen remodel by identifying what does not work in the room, deciding whether the layout will change, and documenting appliances and cabinetry before requesting a detailed proposal.`,sections:[{heading:`Begin with the way you use the kitchen`,paragraphs:[`List the problems you want to solve before collecting finish samples. A crowded cooking aisle, a dishwasher that blocks a cabinet, or a shortage of landing space requires a different response from worn surfaces. Walk through breakfast, grocery unloading, and a family dinner to see where movement becomes difficult.`,`For example, replacing cabinets in their existing positions may improve storage without solving a narrow passage. Moving an island could help circulation, but it can also affect flooring, outlets, lighting, and the ceiling above. Ask for those consequences to be considered together.`]},{heading:`Coordinate appliances, cabinetry and surfaces`,paragraphs:[`Choose appliance dimensions early enough for cabinet planning. Record refrigerator clearances, range requirements, hood locations, and how doors will open. Cabinet drawings should show fillers, panels, organizers, and the location of switches or outlets that might otherwise be concealed.`,`A useful selection sheet identifies the cabinet finish, hardware, sink, faucet, countertop, backsplash, and lighting. Distinguish a confirmed selection from an allowance so that a proposal does not appear more complete than it is. Keep alternate choices available for items with uncertain lead times.`]},{heading:`Create a practical construction plan`,paragraphs:[`Before work begins, agree on protection for adjacent rooms, access for deliveries, storage for appliances, and a temporary food preparation area. Clarify who removes packaging and debris and when the kitchen will be unavailable.`,`Your written scope should explain demolition, trade work, installation, finishing, and cleanup. Ask how site discoveries and requested changes will be documented. A clear decision process is more useful than a schedule that assumes every selection and existing condition is already resolved.`]}],checklist:[`Write down three functional problems.`,`Confirm appliance sizes before cabinet approval.`,`Separate selected products from allowances.`,`Agree on a temporary kitchen and delivery access.`],faqs:[{question:`Should I keep my current kitchen layout?`,answer:`Keep it if it supports the way you cook and move through the room. Compare the functional benefit of a new layout with the additional work it creates before deciding.`}],sources:[]},{slug:`kitchen-remodel-budget-tampa-bay`,category:`Budget & planning`,service:`kitchens-bathrooms`,city:`riverview-fl`,image:`materials`,title:`What Affects a Kitchen Remodel Budget in Tampa Bay?`,description:`Understand the choices that shape a Tampa Bay kitchen remodeling budget, from layout changes and cabinetry to allowances and existing conditions.`,answer:`A kitchen remodeling budget depends on the scope of work, existing conditions, cabinetry, appliance choices, surfaces, and changes to plumbing or electrical systems. A room size alone is not enough to price the work.`,sections:[{heading:`Compare scope before comparing totals`,paragraphs:[`Two proposals can describe a kitchen remodel while covering very different work. One might keep the sink and range in place and reuse flooring; another might move utilities and replace the entire floor. Those totals are not comparable until the inclusions are aligned.`,`Create a simple scope table with a row for demolition, cabinets, countertops, appliances, lighting, flooring, wall repairs, painting, and cleanup. Ask each contractor to identify what is included, excluded, supplied by the owner, or carried as an allowance. This gives you a practical basis for a conversation.`]},{heading:`Understand allowances and unresolved selections`,paragraphs:[`An allowance is a budget provision for an item that has not been fully selected or priced. It should identify what the amount covers and how differences will be reconciled. A countertop allowance that omits fabrication details or installation may not describe the finished cost you expect.`,`For example, a preferred sink or decorative panel can affect both product cost and installation work. Review these details before approving the package. Ask how delivery, storage, taxes, and disposal are handled rather than assuming they are part of every line item.`]},{heading:`Plan for decisions and existing conditions`,paragraphs:[`Opening a wall or removing cabinets can reveal conditions that were not visible during the estimate. Ask how discoveries will be photographed, explained, and priced before additional work proceeds. Keep funds available for agreed changes rather than committing every dollar to finishes.`,`To manage the budget, rank features as essential, desirable, or optional. Preserve the features that solve daily problems first. If an upgraded surface competes with needed storage or electrical work, comparing those priorities makes the decision easier to explain and document.`]}],checklist:[`Compare the same scope across proposals.`,`Identify allowances and owner-supplied items.`,`Ask how changes are approved.`,`Rank upgrades before making selections.`],faqs:[{question:`Can I get an accurate kitchen quote from square footage?`,answer:`Square footage is a starting point, but it does not describe cabinetry, utility changes, product choices, or existing conditions. A project-specific scope is needed.`}],sources:[]},{slug:`bathroom-remodel-planning-clearwater`,category:`Bathrooms`,service:`kitchens-bathrooms`,city:`clearwater-fl`,image:`bathroom`,title:`Bathroom Remodel Planning in Clearwater`,description:`A practical Clearwater bathroom remodeling guide covering layout, storage, shower details, ventilation and the decisions to resolve before work.`,answer:`Plan a Clearwater bathroom remodel around safe movement, shower and vanity dimensions, storage, water management, and ventilation. Resolve these functional details before choosing decorative finishes.`,sections:[{heading:`Measure the room as a working space`,paragraphs:[`Draw the door swing, vanity, toilet, shower, and any storage on one plan. Consider who uses the room and whether people need space to help a child or another household member. A larger vanity is not an improvement if it makes the entry awkward.`,`Check the actual products rather than relying only on a showroom impression. A deep vanity, outward-opening shower door, or projecting cabinet handle changes the space available for movement. Ask the project team to coordinate dimensions before products are ordered.`]},{heading:`Treat the shower as a complete assembly`,paragraphs:[`Tile is one part of a shower. The substrate, waterproofing system, drain, transitions, and penetrations need to work together. Discuss niches, benches, glass attachment points, and the entry detail during planning, before the surfaces are closed.`,`Avoid combining product instructions casually. Ask who is responsible for the complete installation and which manufacturer instructions govern the chosen system. Photos of concealed work and product information can become useful parts of the project record.`]},{heading:`Plan lighting, exhaust and daily storage`,paragraphs:[`Think about the difference between lighting for shaving or makeup and lighting for a quiet evening. Position storage around the items used each day and decide where towels, cleaning supplies, and charging devices belong.`,`Bathroom exhaust is also a planning item. ENERGY STAR describes bathroom fans that discharge outdoors as a way to remove air near its source. Ask the contractor to evaluate the existing exhaust path rather than assuming that a visible grille means the system is suitable.`]}],checklist:[`Check door and shower-door swings.`,`Coordinate waterproofing and drain details.`,`Choose storage around daily routines.`,`Review the exhaust route and lighting plan.`],faqs:[{question:`Should I buy bathroom tile before planning the shower?`,answer:`Confirm the shower layout, drain, installation system, and tile suitability first. Tile size and thickness can affect layout and transitions.`}],sources:[l]},{slug:`walk-in-shower-remodel-planning`,category:`Bathrooms`,service:`kitchens-bathrooms`,city:`st-petersburg-fl`,image:`bathroom`,title:`Planning a Walk-In Shower Remodel in Tampa Bay`,description:`Explore the practical decisions behind a walk-in shower: entry height, drainage, glass, storage, cleaning and coordination with the existing floor.`,answer:`A walk-in shower should be planned as a coordinated floor, drain, waterproofing, and enclosure system. An open appearance does not by itself make a shower easy to enter, comfortable to use, or able to contain water.`,sections:[{heading:`Define what walk-in means for your household`,paragraphs:[`Some homeowners mean a shower with a low entry; others mean a shower without a door or a flush transition. These are different design goals. Explain the actual need, such as easier entry, room for assistance, or simpler cleaning, before selecting the appearance.`,`A conversion from a bathtub to a shower requires a review of the existing space and drain arrangement. Do not assume that removing the tub creates enough room for every enclosure or that a flush entry can be installed without changes below the finished floor.`]},{heading:`Coordinate water containment before glass`,paragraphs:[`Showerhead position, entry location, floor slope, and enclosure geometry affect where water travels. Discuss how the assembly will manage ordinary use, not just how it looks in a rendering. An open entry can require more room than a compact enclosed shower.`,`Ask the team to resolve the drain, waterproofing transitions, and any needed floor buildup before finalizing the glass. Glass dimensions are often confirmed after finished surfaces are in place; ask how that measurement and installation sequence affects use of the room.`]},{heading:`Make room for maintenance and future needs`,paragraphs:[`Place controls where they can be reached without an awkward movement. Consider a hand shower, planned storage, and appropriate reinforcement where future accessories may be desired. The right choices depend on the user and should be discussed individually.`,`Review how to clean the enclosure, drain, and corners. For example, an elaborate niche may offer storage but also introduce more joints and surfaces to maintain. Keep product care instructions with the project information and ask what should be inspected during the final walkthrough.`]}],checklist:[`Describe the entry and access goal.`,`Review drain and floor conditions.`,`Coordinate enclosure measurements.`,`Discuss cleaning and future accessory locations.`],faqs:[{question:`Can every bathroom have a curbless shower?`,answer:`That cannot be determined from appearance alone. Floor structure, drainage, available space, and the complete installation details need an individual review.`}],sources:[]},{slug:`flooring-options-florida-homes`,category:`Flooring`,service:`flooring`,city:`brandon-fl`,image:`flooring`,title:`How to Compare Flooring for Florida Homes`,description:`Compare tile, luxury vinyl and engineered wood for a Florida home by room use, moisture exposure, subfloor preparation and maintenance needs.`,answer:`Choose flooring for a Florida home by matching the product to room use, moisture exposure, subfloor conditions, maintenance, and installation requirements. No single material is the best choice for every room.`,sections:[{heading:`Compare the room, not just the sample`,paragraphs:[`A showroom sample does not show how a floor will meet adjacent rooms, doors, stairs, and cabinets. Start by listing room conditions: pets, tracked-in water, direct sunlight, rolling chairs, and cleaning routines. Compare specific products against those conditions.`,`Tile, luxury vinyl, and engineered wood have different installation and care requirements. Compare the exact manufacturer documentation, including permitted locations and limitations. A water-resistant product description does not establish that the entire room or the layer below the floor is protected from water.`]},{heading:`Include preparation in the flooring scope`,paragraphs:[`Existing flooring removal can expose an uneven surface, old adhesive, or areas requiring repair. Ask how the subfloor will be evaluated and what preparation is included. Material pricing alone does not describe an installed floor.`,`For example, changing from a thick tile assembly to a thinner product can affect baseboards, thresholds, and door clearances. A continuous appearance across several rooms may also require a discussion of manufacturer expansion or transition requirements. Resolve those details before purchasing the quantity.`]},{heading:`Review appearance in your own light`,paragraphs:[`View larger samples in morning and evening light and beside the finishes you plan to retain. Check the pattern variation, grout color where relevant, and how visible dust or footprints may be. Ask for a layout discussion when directional patterns or prominent veining are important.`,`Keep the final scope specific: product name, preparation, installation method, trims, transitions, disposal, and furniture moving. Ask what care products are compatible and when the space can be used again. This helps compare bids without treating different installations as equivalent.`]}],checklist:[`List room exposure and daily use.`,`Read the specific product instructions.`,`Include preparation and transitions.`,`Check samples beside existing finishes.`],faqs:[{question:`Is a waterproof floor enough to solve moisture problems?`,answer:`No. The condition causing moisture and the installation requirements still need evaluation. A product claim does not replace a review of the subfloor or surrounding assembly.`}],sources:[]},{slug:`remodeling-permits-tampa-hillsborough`,category:`Local planning`,service:`remodeling`,city:`tampa-fl`,image:`exterior`,title:`Where to Start With Tampa Remodeling Permits`,description:`Find the right starting point for a Tampa or Hillsborough County remodeling permit and learn what to ask before preparing plans or starting work.`,answer:`Start a Tampa-area permit inquiry by confirming which authority serves the property address and describing the complete proposed scope. A Tampa mailing address alone does not tell you which office reviews the work.`,sections:[{heading:`Confirm the authority for the property`,paragraphs:[`A project inside the City of Tampa and one served by Hillsborough County can follow different application routes. Use the property address when asking where to submit. Avoid applying a permit checklist from one jurisdiction to a project in another.`,`Gather the address, a description of the existing building, and a clear outline of the intended work. If you have existing plans or permit records, identify them for the project team. The aim of this first step is to reach the right office with a useful question.`]},{heading:`Describe the whole scope together`,paragraphs:[`A room name does not fully describe a renovation. Explain whether the proposal changes walls, doors, fixtures, wiring, plumbing, or ventilation. Hillsborough County publishes a submission guide for residential alterations and interior remodeling; the City of Tampa provides its own permit categories.`,`Ask what drawings and supporting information apply to your scope. Do not assume that a neighbor’s project establishes the requirements for yours. Written guidance from the relevant authority and a coordinated project description are better starting points than a generic online answer.`]},{heading:`Include review and inspections in planning`,paragraphs:[`Ask the project team who prepares the application, responds to comments, arranges inspections, and tracks closeout. Keep these responsibilities visible in the written scope. Review time and construction time are different stages and should be discussed separately.`,`Before scheduling demolition or deliveries, confirm the status of the application and what work is authorized. Keep the approved information available to the people doing the work. For a property-specific determination, use the official resources below and contact the relevant department.`]}],checklist:[`Confirm jurisdiction using the address.`,`Describe structural and trade changes.`,`Clarify application responsibilities.`,`Plan inspections and permit closeout.`],faqs:[{question:`Does a Tampa mailing address mean I use City of Tampa permits?`,answer:`Do not assume so. Confirm the permitting authority for the specific property address before following an application route.`}],sources:[s,o]},{slug:`choose-remodeling-contractor-tampa-bay`,category:`Hiring a contractor`,service:`projectmanagement`,city:`tampa-fl`,image:`materials`,title:`How to Compare Remodeling Contractors in Tampa Bay`,description:`Compare Tampa Bay remodeling contractors using license verification, relevant project experience, clear scopes and a documented communication plan.`,answer:`Compare remodeling contractors by verifying credentials, reviewing relevant work, aligning the proposed scope, and asking how communication and changes are handled. The lowest total alone does not establish the best fit.`,sections:[{heading:`Verify the identity behind the proposal`,paragraphs:[`Use the contractor’s exact name and license information when checking credentials. Florida DBPR provides an official license verification process. Make sure you understand which business will sign the agreement rather than relying on a familiar logo or a verbal introduction.`,`Request the relevant insurance information and ask who will manage the work. Keep the verification details with the estimate and correspondence. These steps help establish who is responsible before you spend time comparing decorative options.`]},{heading:`Look for experience relevant to your scope`,paragraphs:[`A portfolio is most useful when you can understand what was actually done. Ask whether a project involved a similar room, occupied home, building type, or coordination challenge. A beautiful finished photo does not explain how a contractor handled access or unexpected conditions.`,`For example, a condominium bathroom and a detached-home bathroom may involve different coordination with building management. Ask how the team would approach the practical conditions at your property. Listen for a process that connects to your project rather than a promise that every job is simple.`]},{heading:`Agree on how the project will be managed`,paragraphs:[`Ask who provides updates, where selections are recorded, and how questions become decisions. A documented change process should explain the effect on scope, cost, and timing before approval. Clarify what happens when a selected item is unavailable.`,`Align the proposals by listing inclusions, exclusions, allowances, owner purchases, protection, and cleanup. Discuss the payment arrangement in the written agreement. Take time to resolve unclear descriptions before signing; clarity at this stage is easier than reconstructing expectations after work begins.`]}],checklist:[`Verify the named contractor through DBPR.`,`Review similar project work.`,`Compare inclusions and exclusions.`,`Confirm the update and change process.`],faqs:[{question:`Where can I check a Florida contractor license?`,answer:`Use Florida DBPR’s official license verification resources and check the identity associated with the proposal and agreement.`}],sources:[c]},{slug:`home-addition-planning-riverview`,category:`Additions`,service:`additions`,city:`riverview-fl`,image:`exterior`,title:`Planning a Home Addition in Riverview`,description:`Plan a Riverview home addition around the property, existing home, room connections, utility needs and a defined design and construction scope.`,answer:`Start a Riverview home addition by defining the space you need and checking how the proposed room connects to the existing home and property. Floor area is only one part of the feasibility discussion.`,sections:[{heading:`Define the problem before choosing the footprint`,paragraphs:[`Decide what the addition needs to do: a quiet bedroom, a larger gathering space, a dedicated office, or a room for a changing household. Describe furniture, storage, light, and privacy needs. A larger rectangle on a plan is not necessarily a better solution.`,`Consider whether reworking existing space could meet part of the need. Comparing that option with an addition helps identify which new area is valuable and which area simply creates more hallway. Include the path from the original home to the new room in the discussion.`]},{heading:`Review the property and existing house together`,paragraphs:[`Ask the project team to consider the site, existing structure, roof connections, drainage, and utility capacity. Locate available surveys and existing plans. Property-specific constraints should be reviewed before treating an inspiration image as a buildable design.`,`For example, a new room that seems simple from inside may require a complicated roof connection outside. The finished floor level, exterior wall transitions, and relationship to outdoor access also affect how the addition feels and functions. These interfaces deserve attention early.`]},{heading:`Plan the connection and occupied-home logistics`,paragraphs:[`An addition may allow some work to occur outside the original footprint, but the tie-in can still disrupt the home. Discuss access, weather protection, temporary separation, and when existing rooms may become unavailable. Confirm the responsibilities for moving and protecting belongings.`,`Request a scope that distinguishes design development, existing-condition review, approvals, construction, and finishing. Ask how utilities and new systems will be coordinated. For projects served by Hillsborough County, use the county’s official residential checklists as a starting point for questions about the submission route.`]}],checklist:[`List the purpose and furniture needs.`,`Gather surveys and existing plans.`,`Review roof, drainage and utility connections.`,`Plan protection during the tie-in.`],faqs:[{question:`Can I plan an addition from a room size alone?`,answer:`No. The site, existing structure, circulation, utilities, and local review process need to be considered with the desired room dimensions.`}],sources:[{title:`Hillsborough County: residential checklists`,url:`https://hcfl.gov/businesses/hillsgovhub/residential-and-mobile-home-checklists`}]},{slug:`remodel-or-build-new-tampa-bay`,category:`New construction`,service:`new-construction`,city:`wesley-chapel-fl`,image:`villa`,title:`Remodel or Build New in Tampa Bay?`,description:`Compare remodeling and new construction in Tampa Bay using the property, existing conditions, design goals, disruption and complete project scope.`,answer:`Choose between remodeling and new construction by comparing how each option meets your goals on the actual property. Review existing conditions, design flexibility, disruption, and the complete scope before comparing costs.`,sections:[{heading:`List what the existing home already does well`,paragraphs:[`Start with the location, room connections, outdoor space, and features you want to keep. Remodeling can be attractive when the basic arrangement works and the changes are focused. It becomes a different exercise when most rooms, utilities, and circulation need to be reconsidered.`,`Write down the problems that cannot be solved by new finishes alone. Low light, a disconnected living area, or a shortage of usable space may require more than cosmetic work. Ask for an option that addresses the underlying problem rather than pricing a list of unrelated replacements.`]},{heading:`Compare complete project boundaries`,paragraphs:[`A fair comparison uses equivalent levels of detail. A renovation estimate that excludes structural work cannot be compared directly with a new-home proposal that includes it. Include design, site work, temporary accommodation, storage, and exterior restoration where they apply.`,`For example, retaining an existing wall may preserve a feature you value while limiting a new layout. New construction may offer more design freedom but introduces its own site and planning questions. Neither option should be judged only by a headline cost per square foot.`]},{heading:`Use a staged decision rather than a guess`,paragraphs:[`Ask the team to identify what must be investigated to compare the options responsibly. That may include existing-condition documentation, a concept layout, or a review of the property. Resolve the uncertainty that could change the decision before developing detailed selections.`,`Set a decision checkpoint once the alternatives have clear inclusions and limitations. Discuss the daily-life impact as well as the finished design: where you will live, what will be stored, and how work will affect access. A useful decision balances the property, the household, and the intended long-term use.`]}],checklist:[`List features worth retaining.`,`Identify problems beyond finishes.`,`Compare equivalent scope boundaries.`,`Include accommodation and disruption.`],faqs:[{question:`Is remodeling always cheaper than building new?`,answer:`No universal answer applies. The extent of changes, existing conditions, site work, and what each proposal includes determine the comparison.`}],sources:[]},{slug:`condo-remodel-planning-clearwater-st-petersburg`,category:`Local planning`,service:`remodeling`,city:`clearwater-fl`,image:`interior`,title:`Planning a Condo Remodel in Clearwater or St. Petersburg`,description:`Prepare for a Clearwater or St. Petersburg condo remodel by coordinating building rules, deliveries, access, shared systems and local permit questions.`,answer:`A condo remodel needs coordination with both the project team and building management. Obtain the building’s renovation instructions, confirm the permitting authority, and resolve access and shared-system questions before scheduling work.`,sections:[{heading:`Get the building requirements in writing`,paragraphs:[`Ask management for its renovation package before finalizing the scope. Review permitted working hours, elevator reservations, delivery routes, protection requirements, and any approval documents. Keep these details with the project information so everyone plans from the same instructions.`,`A unit owner’s preferred start date may depend on building availability. For example, a countertop delivery can require access coordination that differs from an ordinary parcel. Ask who reserves the route, protects shared areas, and communicates changes to management.`]},{heading:`Identify work that touches shared systems`,paragraphs:[`Discuss plumbing shutoffs, exhaust paths, wall penetrations, and other interfaces with the building. Do not assume that everything behind a unit’s finished wall is controlled independently. The project team needs to understand what can be changed and which parties must participate.`,`Flooring and enclosure details can also require building-specific review. Obtain the relevant requirements before ordering products. Keep product documentation available so management and the contractor can evaluate the proposed assembly rather than only a finish name.`]},{heading:`Separate association approval from permit review`,paragraphs:[`Approval from building management does not establish the status of a local permit application. Confirm the authority for the address and ask how the specific work is classified. Pinellas County publishes permitting resources, but the correct route depends on who serves the property.`,`Before work starts, align the approved scope, access plan, delivery schedule, and responsibilities for inspections or building signoffs. Keep a contact list for the owner, contractor, and management representative. This practical coordination can prevent a small scheduling change from becoming a building-wide disruption.`]}],checklist:[`Obtain the renovation package.`,`Confirm deliveries and elevator access.`,`Review shared-system interfaces.`,`Track building and permit approvals separately.`],faqs:[{question:`Does condo association approval replace a permit?`,answer:`Treat them as separate questions. Confirm building requirements with management and permit requirements with the authority serving the address.`}],sources:[{title:`Pinellas County: permitting guide`,url:`https://pinellas.gov/permitting-guide/`}]},{slug:`whole-home-remodel-sequence-tampa`,category:`Budget & planning`,service:`remodeling`,city:`tampa-fl`,image:`interior`,title:`How to Sequence a Whole-Home Remodel in Tampa`,description:`Organize a Tampa whole-home remodel around design decisions, trade coordination, material availability and a practical plan for living through work.`,answer:`Sequence a whole-home remodel by resolving the design and major selections, coordinating work that will be concealed, and protecting finished areas as installation progresses. The exact order depends on the scope and whether the home remains occupied.`,sections:[{heading:`Map decisions before mapping construction days`,paragraphs:[`A schedule depends on more than the date demolition starts. Plans, selections, product dimensions, and delivery information shape what can happen next. List the decisions that affect multiple trades and make them visible before assigning installation dates.`,`For example, a cabinet layout influences lighting, electrical outlets, appliances, and countertops. Changing it after trade work is installed can require rework. Agree on which drawings or selection records constitute approval and how later changes will be handled.`]},{heading:`Coordinate the hidden work before closing surfaces`,paragraphs:[`Ask the project team to explain where plumbing, wiring, ventilation, and structural work intersect. Work behind walls needs coordination with the intended finished layout. Include required review or inspection milestones in the discussion before concealment.`,`Finishes also have dependencies. Some measurements cannot be finalized until supporting work is installed, and some products need time or conditions specified by their manufacturers. A useful schedule explains those dependencies rather than treating every task as interchangeable.`]},{heading:`Choose a realistic occupancy strategy`,paragraphs:[`Living in the home may require phased work, temporary facilities, and safe separation. Working on all rooms at once can reduce the number of repeated transitions, but it may make normal occupancy impractical. Discuss the tradeoff before committing to a start date.`,`Create a room-by-room plan for storage, protected routes, deliveries, cleaning, and access for the household. Confirm how updates will be communicated and what would trigger a schedule revision. A visible decision log helps distinguish a construction delay from time spent waiting for an owner selection.`]}],checklist:[`Track decisions that affect several trades.`,`Identify milestones before concealment.`,`Confirm materials and measurements.`,`Agree on occupancy and protected routes.`],faqs:[{question:`Should I remodel one room at a time?`,answer:`That can help maintain some usable space, but it can also repeat protection and coordination work. Compare phased and combined approaches against the household’s needs and project dependencies.`}],sources:[]},{slug:`prepare-home-remodeling-consultation`,category:`Hiring a contractor`,service:`projectmanagement`,city:`brandon-fl`,image:`materials`,title:`What to Prepare for a Remodeling Consultation`,description:`Make a remodeling consultation more useful with photos, goals, budget priorities, property details and questions about the next planning steps.`,answer:`Prepare a remodeling consultation by gathering a short project description, the property address, useful photos, a budget range, and your preferred timing. You do not need a finished design to begin the conversation.`,sections:[{heading:`Explain the problem in your own words`,paragraphs:[`Describe what is difficult about the current space. Instead of starting only with a style label, explain that the kitchen lacks storage, the bathroom is hard to navigate, or a room needs to serve two purposes. These observations give the discussion a practical direction.`,`Bring a few inspiration images and explain what you like in each one. An image might represent light, cabinet organization, or a material rather than a complete design. Identifying that distinction helps the contractor respond to your actual priorities.`]},{heading:`Gather the information already available`,paragraphs:[`Useful material includes photos of the room and adjacent areas, rough dimensions, existing plans, and product information you have already selected. Label measurements as approximate when they have not been verified. The team can explain which details need a site review.`,`If you live in a condominium or a community with project requirements, obtain those documents too. Share access limitations, parking arrangements, pets, and whether the home will stay occupied. These practical facts can affect planning as much as the finish palette.`]},{heading:`Discuss budget and timing as priorities`,paragraphs:[`Provide a comfortable budget range and say which features matter most. This helps identify whether the goals need to be phased or refined. A preferred completion date is useful context, but the team needs to evaluate the scope and dependencies before confirming a schedule.`,`End by asking what the next step produces: a site visit, additional measurements, design work, or a written proposal. Clarify what information you should supply and how decisions will be recorded. Keep a brief summary so the next conversation builds on the first.`]}],checklist:[`Write the problem you want to solve.`,`Bring photos and approximate dimensions.`,`Share budget priorities and timing.`,`Ask what the next planning step includes.`],faqs:[{question:`Do I need completed plans before calling a contractor?`,answer:`Not for an initial conversation. Explain your goals and ask what design or documentation is needed for the next stage of your particular project.`}],sources:[]},{slug:`remodeling-change-orders-explained`,category:`Budget & planning`,service:`projectmanagement`,city:`lutz-fl`,image:`materials`,title:`Remodeling Change Orders: What to Document`,description:`Learn how to document remodeling changes clearly, including the reason, revised scope, price, schedule effect and the decision needed to proceed.`,answer:`A remodeling change order records an agreed change to the original scope. It should explain the work, its price or pricing basis, the effect on timing, and the required approval before the changed work proceeds.`,sections:[{heading:`Distinguish a change from a clarification`,paragraphs:[`A question about a drawing does not always change the project. A new request, a different product, or additional work caused by a discovered condition may change it. Ask the team to explain which situation applies and where the original scope addressed the item.`,`For example, choosing a different cabinet pull might be a simple selection if it fits the existing allowance and installation. Changing the cabinet layout after approval can affect drawings, fabrication, wiring, and timing. The document should describe the consequence rather than only the new product.`]},{heading:`Record the full effect before deciding`,paragraphs:[`A useful change record identifies the location, reason, work being added or removed, and any supporting photos or sketches. It should make clear whether the price is fixed or based on an agreed calculation. Ask what is included and whether other work must be revised.`,`Include timing in the decision. A product substitution may reduce a purchase price while changing delivery or installation. Keep the approved information with the project documents so the owner and contractor can refer to the same decision later.`]},{heading:`Keep one agreed route for approvals`,paragraphs:[`Choose how changes move from a discussion to an approved instruction. A casual text, jobsite conversation, and revised drawing can otherwise tell different stories. Identify who can authorize a change and how that authorization is retained.`,`Maintain a running list of approved and pending changes. Review the accumulated effect on the project rather than treating each item in isolation. The signed agreement governs the actual process; use the planning conversation to understand it and resolve unclear steps before construction begins.`]}],checklist:[`Identify the reason and location.`,`Describe added and removed work.`,`Document price and timing effects.`,`Retain the agreed approval record.`],faqs:[{question:`Can a small finish change affect the schedule?`,answer:`Yes. A different size, installation method, measurement, or delivery date can affect other work even when the finish looks similar.`}],sources:[]},{slug:`florida-home-renovation-storm-planning`,category:`Florida homes`,service:`remodeling`,city:`sarasota-fl`,image:`exterior`,title:`Storm-Aware Renovation Planning for Florida Homes`,description:`Include the roof, openings, drainage and temporary weather protection in a Florida renovation discussion without relying on unsupported storm claims.`,answer:`Include storm readiness in a Florida renovation by reviewing the condition of the home’s exterior systems and how proposed work affects them. Discuss the roof, openings, water management, and temporary weather protection with the appropriate project professionals.`,sections:[{heading:`Start with the existing exterior systems`,paragraphs:[`Florida’s emergency management guidance highlights the importance of the roof, windows, doors, and related exterior systems. Treat a renovation as an opportunity to identify questions about those systems rather than assuming that new interior finishes address them.`,`Document visible concerns and share available inspection or product records with the project team. A photograph can help describe a question, but it does not establish structural performance. Ask who needs to evaluate the condition and what additional information would support a decision.`]},{heading:`Coordinate improvements rather than isolated products`,paragraphs:[`A replacement opening or exterior change needs to fit the surrounding construction and project requirements. Discuss installation, connections, water management, and the condition of adjacent materials along with the product itself. Avoid treating a marketing label as a guarantee for the whole home.`,`For example, an addition changes how the roof and exterior walls connect to the original house. Ask how those transitions will be designed and reviewed. Any claim about suitability should connect to the actual property and documented installation, not a general statement that all new work is storm proof.`]},{heading:`Plan weather protection during construction`,paragraphs:[`Ask when the building might be temporarily open and what protection is planned for that stage. Discuss material storage, access, and how weather-related decisions will be communicated. Household emergency planning remains important even when construction is underway.`,`Keep the approved plans, product information, and project contacts accessible. If a storm affects the site, coordinate the next steps with the project team before returning to normal work. Use the official Florida preparedness resources for household guidance, and seek property-specific evaluation for construction decisions.`]}],checklist:[`Review exterior-system questions.`,`Gather existing reports and product records.`,`Coordinate connections and water management.`,`Discuss temporary weather protection.`],faqs:[{question:`Does a remodel make my home hurricane proof?`,answer:`No such blanket promise should be made. The condition, design, products, installation, and exposure of the particular home need evaluation.`}],sources:[u]},{slug:`remodeling-final-walkthrough-checklist`,category:`Budget & planning`,service:`remodeling`,city:`palm-harbor-fl`,image:`interior`,title:`A Home Remodeling Final Walkthrough Checklist`,description:`Prepare for a remodeling final walkthrough with a room-by-room review, a clear punch list, product care information and project closeout questions.`,answer:`Use a remodeling final walkthrough to compare the completed work with the agreed scope, record any remaining items, and collect care and closeout information. Keep the review specific and assign a next step to each open item.`,sections:[{heading:`Review one room and one system at a time`,paragraphs:[`Bring the agreed scope and relevant selections to the walkthrough. Move through the space in a consistent order so that a conversation about one detail does not distract from the rest of the room. Check that the installed products match the approved information.`,`Ask for demonstrations of ordinary operation: cabinet drawers, doors, fixtures, lighting controls, and any equipment included in the work. Use the products as intended rather than attempting your own technical inspection. Report anything that does not operate as expected to the project team.`]},{heading:`Make the punch list easy to act on`,paragraphs:[`Describe each remaining item with a location, a short explanation, and a photo where useful. “Hall bathroom, vanity drawer beside the door” is more actionable than “cabinet problem.” Distinguish a completion item from a new request that was outside the agreed work.`,`Agree on who will respond and how the item will be confirmed as complete. If a replacement part is needed, note that dependency. Keep one shared list so that follow-up conversations do not create multiple conflicting versions.`]},{heading:`Collect the information needed after construction`,paragraphs:[`Ask for product care instructions, available warranties, operating information, and relevant project records. Confirm the process for future service questions and retain the correct contact details. Discuss any inspection or permit closeout information that applies to the project.`,`The walkthrough is also a useful time to understand cleaning limitations and when newly installed materials can be used. Follow manufacturer directions for the specific products. Keep the final list and documents together so that maintaining the renovated space is easier after the team leaves.`]}],checklist:[`Bring the scope and approved selections.`,`Demonstrate normal product operation.`,`Record specific remaining items.`,`Collect care, warranty and closeout information.`],faqs:[{question:`What belongs on a remodeling punch list?`,answer:`Record specific remaining or correction items against the agreed scope, with locations and next steps. New upgrades should be discussed separately from completion items.`}],sources:[]}].map(e=>({date:`2026-10-15`,...e,author:`Unique Building Contractors`,readingMinutes:Math.max(1,Math.ceil([e.answer,...e.sections.flatMap(e=>e.paragraphs)].join(` `).split(/\s+/).length/200))}));function f(e){return d.find(t=>t.slug===e)}function p(e){return new Intl.DateTimeFormat(`en-US`,{month:`long`,day:`numeric`,year:`numeric`,timeZone:`UTC`}).format(new Date(`${e}T12:00:00Z`))}export{p as n,f as r,d as t};